1079:**Only the project owner decides these items, OD1 – OD157.** All are answered. OD1 – OD32 (exit 2n, closed) and OD47 were decided on 2026-10-29 (OD23 – OD32 by the strictness pass of that day). OD33 – OD157 were decided on 2026-10-31, by the owner's rule that an answer every reviewer agreed on and as strict as Austral or stricter is taken, and on 2026-11-01, when the owner approved the final set for the rest; the authority decision of the same day supersedes OD38's and OD156's `Root` readings (OD94 was withdrawn: MOD-1 already refuses its case, bug 337). The answers are recorded in [docs/decisions.md](decisions.md), state the decided rules in chapters now (owner revision, 2026-11-03), then become Lean in phases B–D, and are enforced as each new-compiler slice lands. The questions raised while recording them were answered on 2026-11-02, the owner deciding the first four and, by the owner's rule of that day, Claude and Codex agreeing on the best long-term answer for the rest ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02)); they revise OD24, OD38, OD43, OD83, OD102, OD146 and OD156 and the authority rules. The spec audit's questions were answered the same day ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit)), revising OD140 and STMT-13. Traits are exported and imported like any other declaration (owner, 2026-11-02). Core was provisionally approved on 2026-11-05 ([record](#decided-2026-11-05-core)); A2b formalization is current, and the A2c freeze awaits the four design theorems, the A2i integration stage (2026-11-06) and an outside-reader pass. New questions follow the same rule: No agent, orchestrator or
1116:| OD1 | <a id="od1"></a>References: the Austral model. A reference is formed by borrowing a variable or projecting through a reference; `&s.f` is sugar; a temporary is never a root; loans are keyed by the root variable; a raw-pointer dereference needs a trusted validity premise | D2 and §4.5; implemented in landing train 6 (REF-5, REF-11) | exit 2i (G12), so every deliverable; bug 182's repair (exit 0b); V2 (row 6) | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: the Austral model: a reference is formed by borrowing a variable or projecting through a reference; `&s.f` is sugar; a temporary is never a root; references are second-class, never stored or returned; a raw-pointer dereference needs a trusted validity premise; loans are keyed by the root variable (confirmed by OD15 (a), decided 2026-10-29) |
1117:| OD2 | <a id="od2"></a>`&mut` arguments: an implicit reborrow; overlapping exclusive arguments refused; a deferred call keeps its loan until it runs; one defined order for cleanup, loan end and write-back | D1 and §4.5; the reborrow implemented in landing train 6 (REF-10); the defer and exit-order rules not yet | exit 2i (G9, B1, B2), so every deliverable; R-0496 (Core reborrow form) | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: (1) passing a `&mut` on is plain lending, each call's use ending when it returns, with no reborrow concept or syntax; (2) a call is refused when an exclusive argument overlaps any other argument (`h(r, r)`, `h(&mut x, &x)`, `h(&mut a, &mut a.f)` refused); as tightened by OD15 (a) the same day, two arguments that borrow the same root conflict whenever one is exclusive, so even `h(&mut a.f, &mut a.g)` is refused; (3) `&mut` refers to the place, a write through it is the write to that place at once, and copy-in/copy-out is not part of the language (only an optimization proven equivalent); (4) `defer` keeps OWN-12 and a borrow passed to a deferred call stays live until the call runs; (5) one exit sequence on every non-abort exit: the exit value, then the scope's deferred calls (last registered first, innermost scope first), then the scope's loans end, then the remaining locals are destroyed; an abort runs none of it **Canonical current root-based exclusivity rule: REF-11 and OD15(a); this historical overlap formulation is a reference to that rule.** |
1123:| OD8 | <a id="od8"></a>Global state: immutable globals everywhere; mutable state only as declared cells in unsafe-marked modules, reached through capabilities; no `static mut` | D8; pending (TRUST-6, R-0509) | R-0509, R-0496; V6 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: Austral's capability model, extended. (1) Constants only: no mutable global state of any kind (no `static mut`, no cells); a constant is truly immutable, has no interior mutability and never contains a capability, token or handle. (2) Global resources are linear tokens minted at startup: `main` receives a root resource value (in the spirit of Austral's `RootCapability`) and splits it; hardware and runtime singletons (allocator arena, UART and MMIO, panic buffer, errno, C libraries with internal state) are produced or taken only by `trusted fn` wrappers returning or taking linear tokens, and a gate lists those wrappers. (3) Tokens are ordinary values: every effect shows as a token parameter, under the existing loan and exclusivity rules (OD1, OD2, OD15), with no separate effect system. (4) Interrupt handlers, FFI callbacks and future threads get only the tokens explicitly given to them, by installation, transfer or a lock granting a borrow. (5) No life before `main`, no cleanup protocol for global state, and abort runs nothing (OD10). (6) An implicit-context mechanism, if ergonomics later demands one, is a new owner decision, never a hidden global. The cell variant was rejected after the Codex and Grok reviews (interrupts, FFI re-entrancy, linear cell contents, effect polymorphism, concurrency). Now TRUST-8 to TRUST-10 (pending, R-0557) Revised 2026-11-01 – 11-02: items 2 and 3 are superseded by the authority decision (grants handed to `main`; `with(...)` plus resource values); item 5 stands ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority)) |
1125:| OD10 | <a id="od10"></a>Arithmetic: checked by default, named wrapping and saturating operations, shifts with a checked count; an abort runs no deferred call | T-ARITH, recorded as settled (today's behaviour), with §4.5's abort rule | exits 2h and 2j, so V1a, V1b and V1c (checked `+ - *` and their traps); R-0493 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: integer arithmetic is checked by default; wrapping and saturating arithmetic are named operations; the shift count is checked; an abort runs no deferred call **Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): plain arithmetic is admitted only with evidence that it cannot fail; wrapping, saturating and checked (`Result`) forms cover the rest; overflow is still never silent.** |
1130:| OD15 | <a id="od15"></a>G10: whole-owner freezing under a shared borrow stays. G19: no pass assumes `&mut` exclusivity, and the operational loan rules are defined now | T-G10 (keep the freeze) and T-G19 (the policy sentence) | exit 2i (the G10 decision record), so every deliverable; R-0496, R-0505 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner. (a) Whole-owner freezing: a live borrow of any part of a value freezes the whole owner, and loans are keyed by the root binding (REF-11 as in the current compiler); this tightens OD2's part 2: at a call, two arguments that borrow the same root conflict whenever one is exclusive, even disjoint fields (`h(&mut a.f, &mut a.g)` is refused, as in the current compiler); OD1's "keyed by the root variable" stands. (b) The strict option: the language guarantees `&mut` exclusivity, which the checker enforces (OD2, OD15a), and no compiler pass or optimization may assume or exploit it until the checker's guarantee is proven in Lean (R-0499 with the Core semantics R-0496); until then the operational loan rules are defined now, G19 included: a pointer or alias derived from a loan is valid only while that loan lives. Now REF-11@3 (partial, bug 319, which OD23 adds) and REF-14 (pending, R-0496) **Canonical current root-based exclusivity subject: REF-11 (including OD23’s re-lent case); OD2 and OD23 refer here rather than defining separate variants.** |
1138:| OD23 | <a id="od23"></a>REF-11: every argument of a call, the receiver included, is overlap-checked against its root, re-lent `&mut` bindings included (added 2026-10-29, from a Grok review of the spec) | the current compiler exempts a re-lent reference binding (REF-11@2), so `r.add(r.step)` is accepted; bug 319 | exit 2q; R-0499 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner ("strict across the board"): every argument of a call, the receiver included, is overlap-checked against the root, including re-lent `&mut` bindings, so `r.add(r.step)` is refused; read the Copy field into a `let` first. Now REF-11@3 (partial, bug 319) **Canonical current rule: REF-11 and OD15(a); this is the historical clarification of its re-lent case.** |
1168:| OD53 | <a id="od53"></a>Struct update `..base` (added 2026-10-30, item S2 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the struct update form `S { a: 1, ..base }` stays in the language. Options: (a) keep it; (b) remove it, every field written. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b) remove. Austral: no struct update; every field is written | accepted (TYPE-7, OWN-15's value flow for `..base`) | TYPE-7, OWN-15 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove |
1179:| OD64 | <a id="od64"></a>Raw pointers derived from a borrow (added 2026-10-30, item G19 of the owner picks of that day). Question: What a raw pointer derived from a borrow may do: whether it is valid only while the borrow lives, may escape the borrow block, may be passed to C, and how extern parameters say so. Options: (a) valid only while the borrow lives and never escaping it; (b) the operational loan rules of REF-14 only. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a): no returning, no escaping storage (a local `let` inside the block stays allowed), no converting to an integer and no capturing in an outer `defer`; borrow-derived pointers go only to call-only `extern` parameters; C uses it only during a call (memory C keeps must come from an allocation with its own owner); `extern` parameters say call-only (the default) or kept; field pointers come from the same borrow of the whole owner; deferred calls in the block run before the borrow ends; provenance survives copies, offsets, field pointers, helpers and indirect calls; a shared borrow never yields write access; `ptr::expose` is refused on such a pointer; the annotation's syntax gets an LL(1) check. Austral: not checked | no pass assumes `&mut` exclusivity (OD15); REF-14 bounds aliases by their loan | REF-14, TRUST-1, TRUST-3, FFI-3, OWN-12 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Valid only inside the borrow; call-only extern params |
1183:| OD68 | <a id="od68"></a>Failure inside a deferred call (added 2026-10-30, item review new 5 of the owner picks of that day). Question: What OWN-12's text says about a failure inside a deferred call, given that an abort runs no deferred call (STMT-1, STMT-2). Options: (a) OWN-12 states that a trap inside a deferred call ends the program and runs no further deferred call, as STMT-2 and OD10 say for every abort; (b) the remaining deferred calls still run after a trapping one, which would amend STMT-2 and OD10. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (raised by the review). Austral: not checked | failure is abort only (STMT-2); OWN-12's rationale leaves the case open | OWN-12, STMT-1, STMT-2, STMT-11 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Trap ends the program, no further deferred call |
1184:| OD69 | <a id="od69"></a>What "the remaining locals are destroyed" means (added 2026-10-30, item review new 6 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether STMT-11's last exit step ("the remaining locals are destroyed") means storage teardown only, with no synthesized `Destroy` call, as OWN-13 requires. Options: (a) storage teardown only, no call, as OWN-13 requires; (b) a remaining local whose type implements `Destroy` is destroyed by a synthesized call, which would amend OWN-13. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (raised by the review). Austral: no implicit destruction | nothing is destroyed implicitly (OWN-13) | STMT-11, OWN-13 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Storage teardown only |
1212:| OD97 | <a id="od97"></a>A build profile that omits or demands an overflow check (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a build profile may omit the run-time check of an integer operation that no proof discharges, or refuse such an operation at compile time. Bears on ARITH-3, ARITH-9. The current compiler: every profile emits every check, and no option turns one off; ARITH-3's rationale says a profile changes reports, never code generation, and OD10 decided that arithmetic is checked "by default". The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §3.1 instead let a release profile omit a check that is "explicitly configured off and reported", and a provable or high-integrity profile refuse an unproved overflow site. Options: (a) every profile keeps every check that no discharged proof removes; (b) a release profile may turn checks off when configured and reported, never wrapping; (c) as (a), and a profile may also refuse an operation whose no-overflow obligation is not discharged. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-97 | see OPEN-97 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Keep every check; a profile may refuse undischarged ops **Subsumed 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): every build refuses an operation without discharged evidence.** |
1214:| OD99 | <a id="od99"></a>The names of the named conversions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: The spellings of the named conversions of ARITH-5, ARITH-10 and ARITH-11 (`try_convert`, `truncate`, `reinterpret`, the integer-to-float and `f64`-to-`f32` conversions, and the float roundings `trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`). Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10, ARITH-11. The current compiler: none of them exists (E0101). ARITH-5's sentence names `truncate` and `reinterpret`, while the archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 calls its spellings indicative, to be fixed by the API's owner; it names the integer-to-float and `f64`-to-`f32` conversions by their rounding only. Options: (a) the spellings above, as builtins; (b) other names, or std functions rather than builtins. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-99 | see OPEN-99 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): These spellings, as builtins |
1215:| OD100 | <a id="od100"></a>Comparisons on a type parameter (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a comparison on a value of a `Copy`-bounded type parameter is accepted and checked again at each instantiation, or refused in the generic body. Bears on TYPE-6, TYPE-9, PASS-1, OPEN-42. The current compiler: `a < b` with `a, b: T` and `T: Copy` is accepted, and an instantiation at a type with no comparison is refused after monomorphization (E0229, `post-mono`); without the `Copy` bound it is refused in the body (E0229, `check`). TYPE-9's rationale says a generic body is checked once, from its bounds, which a per-instantiation check departs from. Options: (a) keep the current compiler's per-instantiation check; (b) refuse a comparison on a type parameter until a bound states it (a comparison trait); (c) refuse it. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md §8a (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-100 | see OPEN-100 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse until a bound states it |
1216:| OD101 | <a id="od101"></a>What a trap writes (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a run-time trap writes a message naming the operation, its operands and its source location before the process ends, and in what form. Bears on STMT-2, STMT-7, ARITH-3. The current compiler: the binary writes nothing and ends by `SIGABRT`, and `--interp` reports a run-time error naming the trap (`arithmetic overflow (checked +)`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §9.2 described a message `concrete: arithmetic overflow in function 'f' at file:line` with the operation, the operands and the policy. Options: (a) the binary writes nothing; (b) it writes one line naming the trap and its location; (c) the §9.2 form. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-101 | see OPEN-101 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): One line naming the trap and location. Its form, decided 2026-11-06: [runtime row](#decided-project-runtime-2026-11-06) |
1217:| OD102 | <a id="od102"></a>`Float32` and `Char` as type spellings (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether `Float32` and `Char` are other spellings of `f32` and `char`, as `Float64` and `Bool` are of `f64` and `bool`. Bears on TYPE-19, SYN-3. The current compiler: the parser maps `Float64` and `Bool` to `f64` and `bool` but not `Float32` or `Char`, so `fn g(x: f32) -> Float32 { return x; }` is refused (E0220) while the resolver's `tyFromName` maps all four. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §1 names `Float32`/`f32` as two spellings. Options: (a) `Float32` and `Char` become spellings of `f32` and `char`; (b) neither, keeping the current compiler's `Float64` and `Bool`; (c) `Float32` and `Char` decided separately. Whether every capitalized spelling (`Int`, `Uint`, `Float64`, `Bool`) is removed is a separate removal question, not asked here. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-102 | see OPEN-102 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No `Float32` or `Char` aliases. Completed 2026-11-02 by the owner: the aliases `Int`, `Uint`, `Float64` and `Bool` are removed too ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-aliases)) |
1218:| OD103 | <a id="od103"></a>`as` between reference types (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing MEMORY_SEMANTICS). Question: Whether `as` may convert a reference type to a reference type, given that REF-5 lists borrowing and projecting as the only operations that form a reference. Bears on REF-5, REF-6, ARITH-5, TRUST-4. The current compiler: an identity cast between equal reference types is accepted and denotes the operand's place (`read(r as &Int)` with `r: &Int`, and `read(&v as &Int)`, exit 42 on both consumers); every other cast between reference types, `&mut Int as &Int` included, is E0553 at core-check under ARITH-5's tag, whose sentence covers only numeric, `bool` and `char` conversions; a cast from a reference to a raw pointer is allowed (TRUST-4). The design record (MEMORY_SEMANTICS §15.3) listed a cast of a traced reference binding (`r as &U`) among the reference-forming operations. Options: (a) refuse `as` between reference types; (b) allow the identity cast as a no-op on the operand's place. Conversions between a reference and a raw pointer are OPEN-98's, not this question's. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the MEMORY_SEMANTICS absorption, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-103 | see OPEN-103 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse |
1219:| OD104 | <a id="od104"></a>A `#[variant]` written on a counted loop (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the OD47 loop rules). Question: whether a `#[variant]` attribute written on a counted loop is refused, checked like any loop variant, or ignored. Options: (a) refused, the loop's own measure being its only variant; (b) allowed and checked as a loop variant (CON-2); (c) allowed and ignored. No pick given | the counted loop is not in the current compiler (bug 331) | STMT-12, CON-2 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse |
1220:| OD105 | <a id="od105"></a>Where a counted loop's `#[invariant]` is checked (added 2026-10-30, from the same review). Question: where an `#[invariant]` on a counted loop is checked, given that CON-2 checks a loop invariant before the loop and after every iteration. Options: (a) as CON-2 says for every loop; (b) at each entry to the body, so nothing is checked when the loop runs no iteration. No pick given; (b) needs proof requirement 2 | the counted loop is not in the current compiler (bug 331) | STMT-12, CON-2 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): As CON-2: before the loop and after every iteration |
1221:| OD106 | <a id="od106"></a>The result type of a checked conversion (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a checked conversion (`try_convert`) reports a value it cannot preserve as `Option::None` or as a `Result` error carrying the reason. Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10. The current compiler: no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 wrote `try_convert::<T>(x) -> Option<T>` "(or a `Result` with a reason)" and left the choice to the API's owner. Options: (a) `Option<T>`; (b) a `Result` whose error names the reason (out of range, not integral, NaN, infinite, not representable). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500) | ARITH-5, ARITH-10 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `Result<T, OutOfRange>` (failure, not absence) |
1222:| OD107 | <a id="od107"></a>What a float-to-integer rounding gives for NaN, infinities and out-of-range values (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether each float-to-integer rounding operation (`trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`) gives a value or an error for NaN, an infinity or a result outside the target type's range, and which value. Bears on ARITH-1, ARITH-11. The current compiler: the rounding operations do not exist (R-0500); a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range, natively and in `--interp`. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 required a stated result for every input, "either as a value or as an error". Options: (a) an error, as `try_convert` reports; (b) a value: the nearest value of the type for an out-of-range input or an infinity, and 0 for NaN; (c) a trap, as `as` does today. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the rounding operations do not exist; a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range | ARITH-1, ARITH-11 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): An error, as `try_convert` |
1223:| OD108 | <a id="od108"></a>A capability listed twice in a set (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a capability set that lists one capability twice (`with(Console, Console)`) is the same set as the one that lists it once, is refused, or is a different set. Bears on TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TYPE-4. The current compiler: accepted where it is written, and a different type: `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int` is not a `fn(Int) with(Console, Console) -> Int`, in either direction (E0220), because `CapSet.normalize` sorts a set and does not remove a repeated name. The callable-values design ([CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md)) calls the capabilities of a function type a set. Options: (a) the same set; (b) refuse a repeated capability where it is written; (c) a different set, as in the current compiler. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | accepted, and a different set: `with(Console, Console)` is not `with(Console)` (E0220) | TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TYPE-4 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse a repeated capability |
1224:| OD109 | <a id="od109"></a>Specializations that differ only in a function type's capabilities (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether the symbol of a generic function's instantiation includes the capability set of a function-type argument, so that two instantiations that differ only in that set have distinct symbols. Bears on TYPE-17, TYPE-18. The current compiler: one: `pick<T: Copy>` instantiated at `fn(Int) -> Int` and at `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int` emits the single function `pick_for_Fn_T_Int_to_Int_E`, because Mono's `tyToSuffix` drops a function type's capability set while it keeps a struct's capability arguments (E0809's history), and the program runs correctly since capabilities have no run-time representation (TYPE-18). The archived [HANDLE_CAPABILITIES.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/HANDLE_CAPABILITIES.md) R8 required collision-free symbol identity across instantiations and left code sharing to its own argument. Options: (a) the set is part of the symbol, as for struct capability arguments; (b) it is not, one symbol serving both, stated as erasure with its correctness argument. Whether two symbols share one body stays an implementation choice (TYPE-18). Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | one specialization: Mono's symbol drops a function type's capability set | TYPE-17, TYPE-18 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Set is part of the symbol |
1225:| OD110 | <a id="od110"></a>Enums at the foreign boundary (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a value of enum type may cross an `extern fn` boundary, and if so in what representation. Bears on FFI-3, FFI-7. The current compiler: refused: `extern fn f(x: E)` with `enum Copy E { A, B }` is E0574 (a non-FFI-safe parameter); only scalars, raw pointers and `repr(C)` structs cross. The design record [LAYOUT_CONTRACT.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/LAYOUT_CONTRACT.md) says enums must not cross (§3.2); before it was archived it also said they cross as integers. Options: (a) refused, as in the current compiler; (b) a fieldless enum crosses as an integer of a stated width, which needs a stated discriminant for each variant. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | refused (E0574) | FFI-3, FFI-7 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refused |
1226:| OD111 | <a id="od111"></a>Misaligned fields of a packed struct (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a `#[repr(packed)]` struct may hold a field whose alignment exceeds the alignment of its offset, and whether such a field may be borrowed. Bears on FFI-4, FFI-8, REF-5. The current compiler: both accepted: `#[repr(packed)] struct P { v: u8, l: u32 }` places `l` at offset 1, and `&p.l` is accepted and reads the field correctly in the binary and in `--interp`. [LAYOUT_CONTRACT.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/LAYOUT_CONTRACT.md) §2 restricts a packed struct to fields whose alignment does not exceed their position, without saying what enforces it. Options: (a) refuse such a field; (b) accept it and refuse a reference to it; (c) accept both, as in the current compiler. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | both accepted on both consumers | FFI-4, FFI-8, REF-5 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Accept field, refuse a reference to it |
1229:| OD114 | <a id="od114"></a>How integer division rounds (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: How an integer division that does not abort rounds its quotient, and which sign a nonzero remainder takes. Bears on ARITH-3. The current compiler: the quotient is truncated toward zero and a nonzero remainder has the sign of the dividend, as C99 and LLVM's `sdiv` and `srem` give (`-7 / 2` is `-3`, `-7 % 2` is `-1`, `7 % -2` is `1`), in the binary, the interpreter and the constant folder (`IntArith.evalIntBinOp`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) states when division aborts (§5) but not how it rounds. Options: (a) truncation toward zero, as in the current compiler; (b) floored division, the remainder taking the divisor's sign; (c) Euclidean division, the remainder never negative. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the quotient is truncated toward zero and a remainder has the dividend's sign (C99) | ARITH-3 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Truncation toward zero |
1241:| OD126 | <a id="od126"></a>Target assumptions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Which target facts are language rules: 64-bit pointers, little-endian byte order, POSIX hosts, and the sizes of `bool`, `Unit` and pointers. Bears on FFI-2, FFI-7, TYPE-19, TYPE-20. The current compiler: one target profile: `Layout` hardcodes 8-byte pointers and references, `bool` of 1 byte, `Unit` of 0 bytes, an `i32` enum tag, and assumes little-endian POSIX hosts; FFI-2 names AAPCS64 for the native profile, and the integer types' widths follow from their names (TYPE-19). [EXECUTION_MODEL.md](../legacy/docs/platform/EXECUTION_MODEL.md) and [ABI.md](../legacy/docs/platform/ABI.md) state these as platform facts. Options: (a) language rules, a 32-bit or big-endian target being another language profile; (b) target facts, observable only through `sizeof`, `alignof` (FFI-7) and named target constants; (c) as (b), with the sizes of `bool` and `Unit` fixed by the language. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-126 | see OPEN-126 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Language rules; other targets are other profiles |
1262:| OD147 | <a id="od147"></a>`repr(C, packed)` together (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether `#[repr(C, packed)]` is accepted, and what layout it gives. Bears on FFI-4 and FFI-8. The current compiler: accepted: `#[repr(C, packed)] struct Copy P { a: u8, b: u32 }` has size 5, so packing overrides C alignment. FFI-4 refuses only `packed` with `align(N)`, and FFI-8 states C layout and packed layout separately. Options: (a) refuse the combination; (b) accept it as C field order with no padding and alignment 1, as the current compiler does, and FFI-8 says so. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-147 | see OPEN-147 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Accept: C order, no padding, align 1, stated in FFI-8 |
1265:| OD150 | <a id="od150"></a>Which text governs the grammar until it is resynchronized (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether, until OPEN-77's resynchronization, `grammar/concrete.ebnf` or the rule entries govern where they disagree. Bears on SYN-1 and OPEN-77. The current compiler: the parser accepts forms the EBNF does not derive (`import a.{f};`, `m::f()`, `f::<T>(x)`, value `if` and `match`, `pub` fields, bodyless declarations) and the EBNF keeps forms the entries removed (`E#V`, the C-style `for`, `break <expr>`); SYN-1 calls the EBNF the grammar, and the LL(1) checkers read it. Options: (a) the rule entries govern, and the EBNF is descriptive until the resync; (b) the EBNF governs, and an entry that disagrees is pending; (c) neither: each disagreement is decided one by one under OPEN-77. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-150 | see OPEN-150 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Rule entries govern |
1266:| OD151 | <a id="od151"></a>Module paths of more than one level (added 2026-10-31, raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1). Question: Whether a qualified path may name a member of a module nested more than one level deep (`a::b::f()`). Bears on SYN-10, MOD-4 and OPEN-139. The current compiler: the parser's lowercase module-path branch accepts one qualification pair: `a::b::f()`, with `f` in `mod b` inside `mod a`, is a parse error on both consumers (E0001, `expected <, got ident(f)`), because after `a::b` it accepts only `::<` (explicit type arguments). SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1 gives `std::fs::read_to_string` as an example of module qualification, which this parser does not accept. Options: (a) one qualification pair, as in the current compiler, and SYN-10 says so; (b) any depth, every segment before the last naming a module (a repetition, which stays LL(1)). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-151 | see OPEN-151 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Allow multi-level module paths `a::b::f` |
1267:| OD152 | <a id="od152"></a>Whether a path's first letter decides its syntactic form (added 2026-10-31, raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1). Question: Whether the first letter of a path's first segment decides the syntactic form of `X::y`, or one path form leaves to name resolution whether `X` is a type or a module. Bears on SYN-10, SYN-1 and MOD-1. The current compiler: the parser chooses by the first letter of `X`: an uppercase `X` gives an associated-function call or an enum literal, and a lowercase `X` the flattened name `X_y`, which name resolution looks up as one identifier (bug 343); a module named with an uppercase letter goes through the associated-function form, and `M::f()` on `mod M` builds and runs on both consumers. SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1 presents the case split as how the parse stays LL(1). Options: (a) the first letter decides the syntactic form, as in the current compiler, and SYN-10 states it; (b) the parser reads `X::y` as one path form, which is also LL(1), and name resolution decides whether `X` is a type or a module. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-152 | see OPEN-152 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Resolve `X::y` by declaration, not by the first letter |
1268:| OD153 | <a id="od153"></a>Program functions named like a builtin (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Whether a program may declare a function whose name is a builtin's, other than `print`, `println` and `append` (OPEN-133) and the names MOD-3 reserves, and which function the program's calls of that name reach. Bears on MOD-3, MOD-1, MOD-8 and OPEN-133. The current compiler: a program function `string_length` is called by the program's calls on both consumers, and so are program functions named `string_len` and `String_len` (`string_len` returns 7 and `String_len` returns 9; a program that calls both exits 79 on both consumers), while `destroy`, `discard`, `abort`, `alloc`, `free`, `alloc_array`, `free_array` and `realloc_array` are reserved (E0578). The calls the compiler itself generates are bound by identity (MOD-8), so only the program's own calls change. Options: (a) reserve every remaining builtin's name, aliases included, and MOD-3 lists them (`print`, `println` and `append` stay with OPEN-133); (b) allow the declaration, and the program's own calls reach it, as the current compiler does, and MOD-3 says so; (c) allow the declaration, and a program reaches the builtin by a qualified name. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-153 | see OPEN-153 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse program functions named like builtins |
1269:| OD154 | <a id="od154"></a>Whether the string builtins require `Alloc` (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Whether `string_concat`, `string_slice`, `string_substr`, `string_trim`, `int_to_string`, `bool_to_string`, `float_to_string`, `string_push_char`, `string_append`, `string_append_int` and `string_append_bool` require the `Alloc` capability. Bears on MEM-7, TRUST-4 and OPEN-133. The current compiler: Check gives none of these builtins a capability, and a function with no capabilities calls `string_concat` and `string_append_int` and exits 42 on both consumers (the other names were not probed). MEM-7@1 lists `string_reserve` and `append` among the builtins that require `Alloc`; `append` is accepted without `Alloc` (bug 349), and it expands into `string_append`, `string_append_int`, `string_append_bool` and `string_push_char`. MEM-7's rationale says allocation is an effect a signature shows. Options: (a) each builtin named above requires `Alloc`, and MEM-7 lists it; (b) a stated subset of them requires `Alloc`, and MEM-7 lists that subset; (c) only the builtins MEM-7 already lists require `Alloc`, so `string_reserve` and `append` do and the names above do not, and MEM-7 says so; (d) no string builtin requires `Alloc`, `string_reserve` and `append` included, and MEM-7 drops those two. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-154 | see OPEN-154 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Allocating string builtins need `Alloc` (MEM-7; closes bug 349) |
1270:| OD155 | <a id="od155"></a>`unwrap` beside TYPE-24's conversions (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Whether `unwrap` stays an intrinsic beside the conversions TYPE-24 lists (`n.0` and `n as T`), becomes one of those conversions, or is removed. Bears on TYPE-24, TYPE-11, TYPE-10 and OPEN-54. The current compiler: Check gives `unwrap(m)` the newtype's inner type (E0270 when the argument is not a newtype); `return unwrap(m);` exits 42 on both consumers, and `let x: Int = unwrap(m);` followed by `if x == 42` fails the native build at SSA verification (E0715, bug 345). TYPE-24 lists `n.0` and `n as T` and does not list `unwrap`. OPEN-54 decides this only if its answer removes newtypes; while newtypes stay, this question stays. Options: (a) keep `unwrap` as an intrinsic and leave it off TYPE-24's list, as the current compiler and TYPE-24 do; (b) `unwrap(n)` is a conversion, TYPE-24 lists it, and TYPE-11 restricts it as it restricts `n.0`; (c) remove `unwrap`, leaving `n.0` and `n as T`. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-155 | see OPEN-155 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No builtin `unwrap`; use `match` |
1271:| OD156 | <a id="od156"></a>The capability of `get_args` (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Which capability the builtin `get_args` requires, or whether the builtin is removed. Bears on TRUST-4, STMT-7, TRUST-9 and OPEN-136. The current compiler: Check gives `get_args()` the result `HeapArray<String>` and requires `Process` (E0520 with `Env` only); the builtin has no implementation on either consumer (bug 347). STMT-7's rationale says command-line arguments come from `std.args` with `Env`, and OD8 gives `main` the root resource value (TRUST-9). Options: (a) `Env`, as `std.args` requires; (b) `Process`, as the current compiler's signature says; (c) another capability, or none; (d) remove the builtin, and command-line arguments come from `std.args`. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-156 | see OPEN-156 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Superseded by the authority decision of 2026-11-01: `get_args` requires `with(Env)`, and `with(Alloc)` when it allocates. Revised 2026-11-02 by the owner's rule: it also takes the `Environment` grant, and an allocator when it allocates ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-filesystem)) |
1272:| OD157 | <a id="od157"></a>How the type `Unit` is written (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Whether the type `Unit`, which STMT-7 names as a result type of `main` and which other rules and the builtins catalog also name, has a spelling in type positions, and which spelling. Bears on STMT-7, TYPE-4, SYN-1 and OPEN-66. The current compiler: a function with no result type returns no value, and `fn main() { return; }` exits 0; `fn main() -> Unit` and `let x: Unit = f();` are refused at resolve (E0108, unknown type 'Unit'); `fn main() -> ()` is a parse error (E0001, `expected type`). STMT-7@3 says `main`'s result type is `u8` or `Unit`. OPEN-66 concerns the unit value's literal; this entry concerns the type in type positions, and the two cross-reference each other. Options: (a) no spelling: the type is written only by omitting the result type, and STMT-7 says so; (b) `Unit` is a builtin type name, accepted wherever a type is; (c) `()` is the type's spelling; (d) both `Unit` and `()` are accepted. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-157 | see OPEN-157 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): The type is written `Unit`, its value `()` |
1275:## Decided
1288:  suggesting the exact set needed. It supersedes OD38's and OD156's readings through a `Root` value
1302:- <a id="decided-2026-11-02-aliases"></a>**Type-name aliases** (after OD102): the aliases `Int`,
1357:    Abort runs no cleanup and a trapping deferred call stops the sequence (OD68, OD69); these are the
1400:  (OD154) take an allocator and report failure; static text needs no allocation.
1403:the former authority rule 9 of 2026-11-01 on `get_args`, and OD156):
