392:| R-0583 | The per-pass proof-or-validator table | each pass is classified as proved directly (the stable, structural ones: parsing, sequencing, matching, cleanup, representation lowering, BIR → LCB-IR) or checked by a proved validator (the search-heavy or changing ones: specialization, SSA construction and destruction, optimizations, register allocation, scheduling, linking), with the reason; the lexer is proved against an independent byte/token judgment; an unclassified pass fails the gate; the slice-0 rows are delivered at B0 | R-0501, R-0504 |
393:| R-0578 | The unverified LCB build from slice 0, and the cross-backend comparison ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-proof-infrastructure), 2026-11-06) | every supported example is built through BIR → LCB-IR → the Lean Compiler Backend, labelled unverified, beside the LLVM build; CI runs both and compares their full observations (exit status, the fd-2 trap line, startup refusal, later the event traces and external state), never exit codes alone, with each other and with the expected outcome, and any mismatch fails; the LCB output runs natively on a GitHub-hosted AArch64 macOS runner, the first native proof profile's platform; a planted mismatch in either backend is caught | R-0581, R-0503 |
394:| R-0579 | The BIR → LCB-IR adapter refinement proof, the first backend proof target | the adapter's refinement against LCB's imported semantics (not a restatement) is kernel-checked for the slice-0 subset right after the front-end and lowering proofs, at B0, and extended to slice 1 at B1; the end-to-end theorem names the LLVM and LCB premises separately | R-0578, R-0501 |
395:| R-0497 | Immutable pipeline artifacts bound to exact payloads | forgery, mutation and bypass compile-fail controls | R-0514 |
397:| R-0507 | Type and ownership soundness over the executable semantics | proved for slice 1; first applicable increment at B0; the approved slice-1 obligations remain due at B1 | R-0496, R-0499 |
398:| R-0521 | Minimal provenance and one canonical verification task for the first authoritative claims | a claim names its compiler, source, theorem and executable digest; minimal provenance and kernel-checked per-compilation evidence accompany the first authoritative slice-0 claim; the evidence connects the exact input to the exact emitted BIR without trusting native evaluation, the backend input is bound to that BIR, and the Lean compiler, runtime and toolchain are listed as execution assumptions wherever the evidence does not close the gap (2026-11-06) | R-0509 |
399:| R-0552 | Per-compilation certificates (owner-approved 2026-10-25) | a build emits a certificate per output, and the kernel checks it; minimal provenance and kernel-checked per-compilation evidence accompany the first authoritative slice-0 claim; each certificate connects the exact input to the exact emitted BIR through proved judgments or validators, checked by the kernel without trusting native evaluation; the backend input is bound to that BIR (a digest is identity, not correspondence); residual Lean compiler, runtime and toolchain trust is listed as explicit execution assumptions; LCB verification of compiled programs is not counted as verification of the compiler's execution (2026-11-06) | R-0504 |
400:| R-0582 | Per-pass certificates in place of the whole-compiler `decide` certificate | after slice 0, a per-compilation certificate composes universal per-pass theorems with small per-edge witnesses, linear in program size, instead of making the kernel re-run the whole compiler by reduction; slice 0 may keep `decide`; a planted wrong witness at any edge is rejected | R-0552, R-0583 |
402:| R-0573 | Small adversarial interaction workloads from the first slices on, alongside file-copy (decided 2026-11-06) | each slice adds programs that combine its features with earlier ones in difficult ways (for example borrows across cleanup, authority in cleanup, nested views), each with its expected outcome and Lean/native differential check | R-0425, R-0503 |
403:| R-0575 | The per-function facts report and query from slice 0 ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-facts-report), 2026-11-06) | every function of each slice has a report derived from the Lean rules, with a versioned schema: capabilities and the grants and resources actually reachable, allocation and allocator, trusted, foreign and opaque boundaries with their assumptions, each operation that could fail and how it is handled, termination (evidence with its named premises, or the responsible source permission and the call path to it), recursion depth and stack bound or why none is established, proof status per obligation, and completeness, each with its reason, evidence and source location; a permission that justifies nothing is reported as a fact, which a `Concrete.toml` policy may refuse (the decided refusals of an unmatched `#[proof]` and a duplicate `#[may_not_terminate]` stay) (carried from slice 0 into CheckedCore and the schema, spec obligations O1 and O4); the schema already has fields for what memory a function writes (frames) and what its outputs depend on (dependency flow), reported "not established" until derived: definite, maybe and never writes per `&mut` place and the output-to-input dependencies (control dependence, cleanup and opaque calls included) are derived into these fields as soon as `&mut` lands, not in E–G, while R-0524, R-0473 and R-0383 keep source frames, contracts and package summaries; facts are preserved across indirect calls, specialization and package boundaries (R-0588); from slice 0 the compiler answers a query about one function or module from the same facts, as readable text and in the machine format (the command is `concrete facts`); the report never claims what the rules do not establish; every fact is a typed judgment in Lean with a derivation-soundness theorem (and obligation-discovery completeness where it claims absence), one derivation feeds the readable view, the machine format and every policy, each fact has a closed status (established, conditional, refuted, not established, not applicable) with per-fact coverage, unknown never satisfies a policy, and answers carry typed identities, versioned schema and semantics and artifact digests; from slice 0 the report also carries the build identity (compiler SHA, toolchain pins and target), each program's maximum stack use, measured from the final binary on both backends, and the trusted-base statement naming the Lean kernel, LLVM printer and LLVM, LCB's MIR→assembly step, linker, OS and named runtime premises as not proven ([early decisions](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-early-evidence)); the diagnostics and facts JSON schema is frozen and versioned from the first front-end output, with golden tests; a planted wrong fact and a planted unknown version are caught; Home: spec (the stack-bound claim and, from slice 0, the refusal of an unknown or over-budget bound against the `Concrete.toml` stack budget) and tooling (the report). Gate: stack, the final-artifact bound checked against the claim and the budget ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0499 |

