legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md:107:**ARITH-3@1.** `+`, `-`, `*`, unary `-`, `/` and `%` on integers abort when the mathematical result is not representable in the operand type, when the divisor is zero and for `MIN / -1` and `MIN % -1`, and a shift aborts when its count is negative or at least the operand's width and otherwise discards the bits shifted out.
legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md:122:fill of a right shift that does not abort is ARITH-8; OD114 makes integer division truncate toward zero and the remainder take the dividend’s sign. ARITH-13 refuses an operation the compiler proves always overflows (E0902), but in the current
legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md:132:Decided by the owner on 2026-10-29 (OD10): integer arithmetic is checked by default and the shift count is checked; wrapping and saturating arithmetic are named operations (ARITH-4), and an abort runs no deferred call (STMT-2).
legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md:178:```con abort name=shift-count-too-large trap=shift_amount_out_of_range
legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md:196:**ARITH-4@1.** `wrapping_add`, `wrapping_sub` and `wrapping_mul` compute the two's-complement result modulo 2 to the operand's width, and `saturating_add`, `saturating_sub` and `saturating_mul` clamp it to the type's range, for every integer type and without a trap.
legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md:209:saturating division and no wrapping shift; division has no modular meaning, and a shift by
legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md:300:### ARITH-6. Comparisons follow their operand type
legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md:302:**ARITH-6@1.** An integer comparison is signed or unsigned as its operand type is, a float comparison follows IEEE 754 (every ordered comparison with a NaN operand is false, and `x == x` is false for a NaN), and a comparison reads its operands without moving them.
legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md:384:### ARITH-8. The fill of a right shift
